title: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/tiddlers/sigil-lele
tags: $:/tags/Global lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/SharktoothSigil
lar-kind: concurrency
lar-pono-uri: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lele
lar-see-also: \branch
lar-pattern: <<~\s*lele\s+to=`([^`]*)`\s*>>
lar-example: <<~ lele to=`lar:///a.b.c/x`>>
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<!-- lele — a SIDE FLOW at a target, and the caller carries on. THE TARGET RIDES AS
     `to`, one NAMED, TICKED value:

         <<~ lele to=`lar:///a.b.c/x`>>

     //lele// leaps and knows the tree it left. The edge emits into the message family, starts the
     target flow, and waits for no answer; the enclosing async context bounds the leap and cancels
     what remains when that context completes. `hoolele` makes the other move — it leaves the tree.

     A BARE ADDRESS REACHES NOTHING. `lar:///x` standing bare binds a parameter named `lar` and the
     slot receives nothing; `to=` and one pair of ticks fill it.

     THE PAGE STAYS CLEAR. A side flow governs execution and never layout, so the definition hands
     the compile layer a data annotation carrying the target and renders nothing. -->

\procedure ~lele(to:"")
<span class="lar-lele" data-lele-target=<<to>> data-bound="enclosing-scope" hidden="true"/>
\end
